    "rules": [
      "All term groups belong to one standalone category, Biological Terrorism. They are retrieval roles, not map subcategories.",
      "Core phrases retrieve candidates, not automatic map events. Exclude generic policy, exercises, research, commentary and historical retrospectives unless they report a new in-scope incident or judicial development.",
      "Agent and disease names require related incident context in the title or the same relevant passage. An unrelated terrorism paragraph elsewhere in an article is insufficient.",
      "Food or water contamination, agricultural sabotage and suspicious powders are not automatically biological. Establish reported biological involvement or an explicitly biological threat before assignment.",
      "Retain suspected plots, threats, hoaxes, investigations and judicial updates when within scope, but preserve the reported status. Never label a suspicious powder as a confirmed biological agent without supporting evidence.",
      "A terrorism nexus must be reported or supported by credible evidence. Ordinary crime, natural outbreaks, accidental contamination and laboratory accidents do not become terrorism because a pathogen is mentioned.",
      "Theft or trafficking without a reported terrorism nexus may be reviewed as a candidate but must not be published as biological terrorism automatically.",
      "Some toxins overlap chemical and biological classifications. Use one canonical incident identifier; relevant filters may share that record without inflating overall incident counts.",
      "Apply word boundaries, punctuation and case normalization, and appropriate language-specific normalization. Short acronyms and ambiguous words must not trigger on substrings.",
      "English, French and Arabic seed phrases are included. Additional languages and aliases require colleague validation; this is not a claim of exhaustive global coverage."
    ],
